messages:
Mina N: Quick q — we should pick a single HTTP client story for the JS SDK v2 (undici vs global fetch) + a polyfill pattern that works across ESM/CJS and edge runtimes. I'm seeing onboarding friction in sample apps where people hit missing fetch or bundler errors. leaning: prefer global fetch when present, lazy-load undici in Node. thoughts on typings & CJS interop?

Ethan (eng-sdk): +1 to prefer global fetch. Implementation notes: lazy import undici only when needed so edge/worker bundles don't pull it in. Also we should provide a tiny adapter in the SDK so users never see the branching. Example: ````
const getFetch = async () => {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  try {
    const { fetch } = await import('undici');
    return fetch;
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error('No fetch available. Install a polyfill or run on Node >=18.');
  }
};
export default getFetch;
````
This avoids require() which can break ESM bundling.

Priya: Two gotchas I hit in sample apps: 1) Next dev sometimes polyfills fetch differently in edge vs serverless which changes streaming behavior, 2) TypeScript: consumers get mismatched Response/Request types if we rely on undici types vs lib.dom. We should force the SDK to use the web fetch type surface in d.ts (lib.dom) and avoid exporting undici-specific types.

Samir: For CJS consumers we should provide an example snippet in README for compatibility: ``const { createClient } = require('@redwood/inference'); // uses lazy fetch adapter`` so people don't have to change runtime. Also add a small runtime detection helper that uses `typeof globalThis.fetch` first — that works inside many bundlers.

Tomas: CI plan — add a matrix job that runs the SDK tests under: Node 16 (with undici installed), Node 18 (native fetch), Node 20, and an Edge simulated runtime (playwright + worker globals or bun?). That will catch subtle differences early. Also add an e2e sample app that runs in both Next server and Next edge functions to exercise streaming/fetch behaviors.

Ethan (eng-sdk): Typing proposal: ship our d.ts where the public API types only reference standard web Fetch types (Request/Response/BodyInit). Add a small compatibility note: if a consumer uses undici-specific options (like dispatcher) they must cast or import undici types themselves. Avoid adding undici as a transitive type dependency.

Priya: Bundlers: ensure undici import is behind a dynamic import so tools like webpack/rollup/next's bundler can tree-shake it away for edge builds. Also add a note for Vite: ``optimizeDeps.exclude = ['undici']`` in sample app for local dev to avoid prebundling surprises.

Mina N: Nice — can we capture a minimal doc + code sample and a small DX PR that: 1) adds adapter helper in sdk-core, 2) updates README and samples, 3) CI matrix job? I can own the docs + sample app. Who wants the adapter PR? :hand:

Ethan (eng-sdk): I'll take the adapter PR. Plan: small package fn getFetch() in runtime-utils, unit tests that stub globalThis.fetch and a test that installs undici in the fixture. I'll also add a TypeScript declaration file that re-exports fetch types in terms of lib.dom.

ci-bot: Job queued: runtime-matrix/detect-fetch (queued by Ethan).

Samir: FYI for release note — mention "Better runtime fetch detection + undici fallback" and link sample app. Also we'll keep an onboarding checklist in the README for common gotchas (Next edge vs server, Vite prebundle, TS lib dom).

Mina N: Great — created a docs issue and added checklist items. PR owners: Ethan for adapter, Mina for docs/sample. We'll iterate after CI results. :rocket:
